Figure 2 characterizes the physical properties of Nano-11 nanoparticles, demonstrating their suitability as vaccine adjuvants. Transmission electron microscopy (A) reveals spherical particles with an irregular, cauliflower-like appearance. Dynamic light scattering (B) indicates a uniform hydrodynamic diameter of approximately 75-80 nm. The nanoparticles exhibit a stable positive zeta potential (C) ranging from +7 to +15 mV across a wide pH range. This positive surface charge facilitates the dose-dependent adsorption of negatively charged ovalbumin (D), a protein antigen, while showing negligible adsorption of positively charged lysozyme, supporting electrostatic interactions vital for antigen delivery.